arm64ec: a notification hook must not block, and must not touch TLS

Resident Evil Requiem deadlocks about twenty seconds in with CPU and GPU both
flat at 0%. Wine's critical-section timeout diagnostics name the cycle:

    section 00000000004500D0 "ntdll/heap.c: main process heap section"
        wait timed out in thread 0280, blocked by 0188
    section 0000006FFE04D498 "?"
        wait timed out in thread 0188, blocked by 0280

The unnamed section is FEX's. libarm64ecfex.dll mapped at 0x6FFDCB0000 and is
5,148,672 bytes, so 0x6FFE04D498 lies 0x39D498 into its data, and
ThreadCreationMutex is the std::recursive_mutex there -- recursive being what
an RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION is, and RtlpWaitForCriticalSection what waits on one.

Wine calls the memory and read notifications as a before/after pair around the
syscall, so locking in the "before" half holds the mutex across
NtAllocateVirtualMemory and friends. Two opposite orders result:

  0188  heap section held, heap grows, NtAllocateVirtualMemory calls
        NotifyMemoryAlloc(After=FALSE) -- blocks on ThreadCreationMutex
  0280  loader section held, a notify hook takes ThreadCreationMutex, and the
        syscall Wine then performs allocates -- blocks on the heap section

try_lock in the "before" half breaks the edge Wine forces from inside the
syscall, and breaking either edge is enough. Nothing is skipped when the try
fails: the notification still runs, and the interval map stays consistent
because InvalidationTracker locks IntervalsLock around every mutation of it.
What is given up is coarse serialisation against thread creation, which
narrows to a race the two halves of the pair already allowed.

**The record of which nesting level acquired must not live in TLS.** The first
version of this patch used thread_local for it and made things worse rather
than better: re9.exe took an access violation at 0x7FFFB86E14 -- inside
libarm64ecfex.dll, which was mapped at 0x7FFFA80000 -- with only two modules
loaded, so during emulator startup rather than anywhere near the game. These
hooks run from the very first NtAllocateVirtualMemory, which is earlier than
TLS is usable for a module the BT layer loads. FrontendThreadData is where FEX
already keeps per-thread state on exactly this path: BTCpu64NotifyReadFile
holds InLockedRWXRead there across the same before/after pairing.

Per nesting level rather than one flag, because the pairs nest and a failed try
at one level must not release what another level took.

Not addressed, deliberately: BTCpu64NotifyReadFile also holds
CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex() across the read, and NotifyMapViewOfSection
still blocks on a scoped_lock. Neither was in the measured cycle, and neither
can be made non-blocking without dropping work that has to happen.

VESSEL-LOCAL. native/fex/CLAUDE.md forbids AI-generated code in contributions
to FEX, so this must not be sent upstream.

diff --git a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
index ab3e7a35e..7a80e828b 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
@@ -132,6 +132,17 @@ struct ThreadCPUArea {
 
 struct FrontendThreadData {
   bool InLockedRWXRead {};
+
+  // VESSEL: which nesting levels of a memory-notification pair actually took
+  // ThreadCreationMutex. See NotifyLockEnter for why those pairs cannot block.
+  //
+  // Here rather than in a thread_local: libarm64ecfex.dll is loaded by the BT
+  // layer and these hooks run from the very first NtAllocateVirtualMemory,
+  // which is earlier than TLS for this module is usable. A first attempt used
+  // thread_local and faulted inside FEX during startup with two modules mapped.
+  // InLockedRWXRead above already lives here across the same pairing.
+  uint64_t NotifyLockAcquired {};
+  uint32_t NotifyLockDepth {};
 };
 
 namespace {
@@ -148,6 +159,67 @@ std::recursive_mutex ThreadCreationMutex;
 // Map of TIDs to their FEX thread state, `ThreadCreationMutex` must be locked when accessing
 std::unordered_map<DWORD, FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState*> Threads;
 
+// VESSEL: the memory notification hooks must never *block* on the way in.
+//
+// Wine calls NotifyMemory{Alloc,Free,Protect}, NotifyUnmapViewOfSection and
+// BTCpu64NotifyReadFile as a before/after pair around the syscall itself, so
+// locking in the "before" half holds the mutex across NtAllocateVirtualMemory
+// and friends. That puts FEX on both sides of a lock order it does not own,
+// and the two orders are opposite:
+//
+//   thread A  RtlAllocateHeap takes the process heap section, the heap needs to
+//             grow, NtAllocateVirtualMemory calls NotifyMemoryAlloc(After=FALSE)
+//             -- blocks on ThreadCreationMutex
+//   thread B  inside LdrLoadDll (loader section held) enters a notify hook and
+//             takes ThreadCreationMutex; the syscall Wine then performs itself
+//             allocates -- blocks on the process heap section
+//
+// Measured on device with Resident Evil Requiem loading DStorageCore.dll:
+//
+//   section ...4500D0 "heap.c: main process heap section" blocked by 0188
+//   section ...E04D498 "?"  (this mutex)                  blocked by 0280
+//
+// 0280 held the loader section and this mutex while waiting on the heap, 0188
+// held the heap while waiting on this mutex, and 0184/01f8/0284 queued behind
+// them. CPU and GPU both flatlined at 0%.
+//
+// try_lock breaks the edge Wine forces from inside a syscall, and breaking
+// either edge is enough. When the try fails the notification still runs, which
+// is safe for the interval map -- InvalidationTracker takes IntervalsLock
+// around every mutation of it. What is given up is coarse serialisation against
+// thread creation, narrowing to a race the two halves already allowed.
+//
+// Recorded per nesting level, not with one flag: the pairs nest, and a failed
+// try at one level must not release what another level took.
+constexpr uint32_t NotifyLockMaxDepth = 64;
+
+FrontendThreadData* GetFrontendThreadData(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread);
+
+// Enter the "before" half of a notification pair without ever blocking.
+void NotifyLockEnter(FrontendThreadData* Data) {
+  const uint32_t Level = Data->NotifyLockDepth++;
+  if (Level >= NotifyLockMaxDepth) {
+    return;
+  }
+  if (ThreadCreationMutex.try_lock()) {
+    Data->NotifyLockAcquired |= (1ull << Level);
+  } else {
+    Data->NotifyLockAcquired &= ~(1ull << Level);
+  }
+}
+
+// Leave the "after" half, releasing only if this level actually acquired.
+void NotifyLockLeave(FrontendThreadData* Data) {
+  if (!Data->NotifyLockDepth) {
+    return;
+  }
+  const uint32_t Level = --Data->NotifyLockDepth;
+  if (Level < NotifyLockMaxDepth && (Data->NotifyLockAcquired & (1ull << Level))) {
+    Data->NotifyLockAcquired &= ~(1ull << Level);
+    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+  }
+}
+
 std::pair<NTSTATUS, ThreadCPUArea> GetThreadCPUArea(HANDLE Thread) {
   THREAD_BASIC_INFORMATION Info;
   const NTSTATUS Err = NtQueryInformationThread(Thread, ThreadBasicInformation, &Info, sizeof(Info), nullptr);
@@ -784,13 +856,13 @@ void NotifyMemoryAlloc(void* Address, SIZE_T Size, ULONG Type, ULONG Prot, BOOL
   }
 
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter(GetFrontendThreadData(GetCPUArea().ThreadState()));
   } else {
     // MEM_RESET(_UNDO) ignores the passed permissions
     if (!Status && !(Type & (MEM_RESET | MEM_RESET_UNDO))) {
       InvalidationTracker->HandleMemoryProtectionNotification(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), static_cast<uint64_t>(Size), Prot);
     }
-    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+    NotifyLockLeave(GetFrontendThreadData(GetCPUArea().ThreadState()));
   }
 }
 
@@ -800,12 +872,12 @@ void NotifyMemoryFree(void* Address, SIZE_T Size, ULONG FreeType, BOOL After, NT
   }
 
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter(GetFrontendThreadData(GetCPUArea().ThreadState()));
   } else {
     if (!Status) {
       InvalidationTracker->InvalidateAlignedInterval(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), static_cast<uint64_t>(Size), true);
     }
-    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+    NotifyLockLeave(GetFrontendThreadData(GetCPUArea().ThreadState()));
   }
 }
 
@@ -815,12 +887,12 @@ void NotifyMemoryProtect(void* Address, SIZE_T Size, ULONG NewProt, BOOL After,
   }
 
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter(GetFrontendThreadData(GetCPUArea().ThreadState()));
   } else {
     if (!Status) {
       InvalidationTracker->HandleMemoryProtectionNotification(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), static_cast<uint64_t>(Size), NewProt);
     }
-    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+    NotifyLockLeave(GetFrontendThreadData(GetCPUArea().ThreadState()));
   }
 }
 
@@ -844,13 +916,13 @@ void NotifyUnmapViewOfSection(void* Address, BOOL After, NTSTATUS Status) {
   }
 
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter(GetFrontendThreadData(GetCPUArea().ThreadState()));
     auto [Start, Size] = InvalidationTracker->InvalidateContainingSection(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), true);
     if (Size) {
       HandleImageUnmap(Start, Size);
     }
   } else {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+    NotifyLockLeave(GetFrontendThreadData(GetCPUArea().ThreadState()));
   }
 }
 
@@ -889,19 +961,19 @@ void BTCpu64NotifyReadFile(HANDLE Handle, void* Address, SIZE_T Size, BOOL After
 
   auto& InLockedRWXRead = GetFrontendThreadData(ThreadState)->InLockedRWXRead;
   if (!After) {
-    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    NotifyLockEnter(GetFrontendThreadData(ThreadState));
     CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex().lock();
     if (InvalidationTracker->BeginUntrackedWriteLocked(reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Address), static_cast<uint64_t>(Size))) {
       InLockedRWXRead = true;
     } else {
       CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex().unlock();
-      ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+      NotifyLockLeave(GetFrontendThreadData(ThreadState));
     }
   } else {
     if (InLockedRWXRead) {
       InLockedRWXRead = false;
       CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex().unlock();
-      ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+      NotifyLockLeave(GetFrontendThreadData(ThreadState));
     }
   }
 }
